1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,843
Imagine getting the keys to an
apartment and owing ₩0 in monthly rent.

2
00:00:05,843 --> 00:00:07,763
There is one condition.

3
00:00:07,763 --> 00:00:13,272
Before moving in, you hand the
landlord a deposit of ₩200,000,000.

4
00:00:13,722 --> 00:00:17,487
Now for the part that makes
this lease surprising.

5
00:00:17,487 --> 00:00:22,306
When it ends, the landlord owes
you that same ₩200,000,000 back.

6
00:00:22,306 --> 00:00:27,426
The usual deal is that you return
the keys and get the deposit back.

7
00:00:27,876 --> 00:00:31,081
The deposit isn't an
advance payment of rent.

8
00:00:31,081 --> 00:00:34,643
It pays for the arrangement
by giving the landlord

9
00:00:34,643 --> 00:00:38,062
use of the money while
you have use of the home.

10
00:00:38,062 --> 00:00:41,196
In Korea, that arrangement
is called jeonse.

11
00:00:41,646 --> 00:00:46,710
The same deposit connects all
3 stages of a jeonse lease.

12
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:50,331
Start with what the
contract gives each side.

13
00:00:50,331 --> 00:00:54,911
The tenant gets the right to live
in the home for the lease term.

14
00:00:54,911 --> 00:01:00,336
The landlord keeps ownership and takes
on an obligation to repay the deposit.

15
00:01:00,786 --> 00:01:03,816
A Korean monthly rental is called wolse.

16
00:01:03,816 --> 00:01:08,437
It commonly combines a deposit
with a recurring rent payment.

17
00:01:08,437 --> 00:01:11,997
With pure jeonse, the
deposit is typically much

18
00:01:11,997 --> 00:01:15,330
larger, and the recurring
rent line is zero.

19
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:19,159
Some leases sit between
those two arrangements.

20
00:01:19,159 --> 00:01:22,825
They pair a sizable deposit
with some monthly rent.

21
00:01:22,825 --> 00:01:26,347
The difference is visible
in the contract itself.

22
00:01:26,347 --> 00:01:30,876
Look at both the upfront deposit
and the amount due each month.

23
00:01:31,326 --> 00:01:36,300
Let's use a single imaginary
apartment to follow the money.

24
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:40,768
The terms in this example
are a ₩200,000,000 deposit,

25
00:01:40,768 --> 00:01:44,814
₩0 in monthly rent, and
a lease lasting 2 years.

26
00:01:45,264 --> 00:01:49,781
There can be more than one payment
before the tenant settles in.

27
00:01:49,781 --> 00:01:53,098
Seoul's housing guide
describes a common split.

28
00:01:53,098 --> 00:01:58,320
Part of the deposit is paid at signing,
and the rest comes due at move-in.

29
00:01:58,770 --> 00:02:01,542
Say the signing payment is 10%.

30
00:02:01,542 --> 00:02:06,014
On our imaginary lease,
that makes it ₩20,000,000.

31
00:02:06,014 --> 00:02:10,127
That payment counts
toward the agreed deposit,

32
00:02:10,127 --> 00:02:13,794
so the remaining balance is ₩180,000,000.

33
00:02:14,244 --> 00:02:19,551
At move-in, the balance brings
the total paid to ₩200,000,000.

34
00:02:19,551 --> 00:02:21,948
The tenant now has the keys.

35
00:02:22,398 --> 00:02:27,752
Two years is also a useful reference
point in Korean housing law.

36
00:02:27,752 --> 00:02:32,446
A housing lease with no
specified term, or a term shorter

37
00:02:32,446 --> 00:02:36,894
than 2 years, is generally
treated as lasting 2 years.

38
00:02:37,344 --> 00:02:40,106
The law gives the tenant a choice here.

39
00:02:40,106 --> 00:02:45,912
If the contract specifies a shorter term,
the tenant can assert that shorter term.

40
00:02:46,362 --> 00:02:50,770
The absence of monthly rent
only describes the rent line.

41
00:02:50,770 --> 00:02:55,410
Electricity, water, and building
charges can still come due.

42
00:02:55,860 --> 00:03:00,008
During the lease, the landlord
still owes the agreed sum.

43
00:03:00,008 --> 00:03:04,812
The lease does not shave a month's
worth of rent off that balance.

44
00:03:05,262 --> 00:03:09,554
So what does the landlord get from
a lease with zero monthly rent?

45
00:03:09,554 --> 00:03:14,886
The answer is the use of a large sum of
money for the duration of the arrangement.

46
00:03:15,336 --> 00:03:20,215
For example, the deposit could be
used to pay down part of a loan.

47
00:03:20,215 --> 00:03:23,986
That can reduce interest
expenses during the lease.

48
00:03:23,986 --> 00:03:26,721
The obligation to return the tenant's

49
00:03:26,721 --> 00:03:30,048
deposit remains in place
through all of that.

50
00:03:30,498 --> 00:03:34,083
For the tenant, the
deposit is money owed back.

51
00:03:34,083 --> 00:03:39,498
For the landlord, it is money available
now with a repayment due later.

52
00:03:39,948 --> 00:03:43,462
A tenant who funds the
deposit from savings gives up

53
00:03:43,462 --> 00:03:46,908
whatever those savings
could have earned elsewhere.

54
00:03:47,358 --> 00:03:51,538
Suppose the money could
earn 3% a year before tax.

55
00:03:51,538 --> 00:03:54,881
On a ₩200,000,000 deposit, that would be

56
00:03:54,881 --> 00:03:58,559
₩6,000,000 in foregone
interest over a year.

57
00:03:58,559 --> 00:04:02,070
That's an illustration, not a market rate.

58
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:08,746
Spread that hypothetical interest over
12 months and you get ₩500,000 a month.

59
00:04:08,746 --> 00:04:11,540
Nobody sends a monthly bill for it.

60
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:16,968
It is the value of the interest the
tenant gives up in this example.

61
00:04:17,418 --> 00:04:20,437
If the tenant borrows part of the deposit,

62
00:04:20,437 --> 00:04:23,672
there may be a real
interest payment as well.

63
00:04:23,672 --> 00:04:25,972
That payment goes to the lender.

64
00:04:25,972 --> 00:04:28,847
It is the cost of financing the deposit,

65
00:04:28,847 --> 00:04:32,010
separate from the rent
owed to the landlord.

66
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:35,607
To compare costs, add monthly rent, loan

67
00:04:35,607 --> 00:04:39,226
interest, and the interest
forgone on savings.

68
00:04:39,226 --> 00:04:42,924
Identical apartments can
carry different costs.

69
00:04:43,374 --> 00:04:45,827
There is also a question of access.

70
00:04:45,827 --> 00:04:49,751
A household can have enough
income to handle the ongoing

71
00:04:49,751 --> 00:04:53,466
expenses and still need time
to assemble the deposit.

72
00:04:53,466 --> 00:04:57,390
Jeonse puts that funding
requirement right at the front.

73
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:01,764
At the end of the lease, the
deposit moves from an amount

74
00:05:01,764 --> 00:05:05,894
written in the contract to money
that needs to be paid back.

75
00:05:05,894 --> 00:05:10,368
The outgoing tenant may already
need that money for another home.

76
00:05:10,818 --> 00:05:14,156
In our simple example,
the tenant hands the

77
00:05:14,156 --> 00:05:18,271
apartment back and the
landlord returns ₩200,000,000.

78
00:05:18,271 --> 00:05:21,066
The deposit has made its round trip.

79
00:05:21,516 --> 00:05:24,252
The timing of that last step matters.

80
00:05:24,252 --> 00:05:29,353
A refund can be due on one home just
as a deposit is due on the next.

81
00:05:29,353 --> 00:05:35,268
Even a delay can leave a tenant with a
funding gap between move-out and move-in.

82
00:05:35,718 --> 00:05:39,514
A landlord might repay
from available cash, from

83
00:05:39,514 --> 00:05:42,835
borrowing, or from a new tenant's deposit.

84
00:05:42,835 --> 00:05:45,998
The lease obliges the landlord to repay.

85
00:05:45,998 --> 00:05:50,742
A new tenant's arrival is just
one possible source of funds.

86
00:05:51,192 --> 00:05:54,167
Say the next tenant's deposit is smaller.

87
00:05:54,167 --> 00:05:59,100
That new payment alone won't cover
the refund owed on the old lease.

88
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:02,510
The difference has to
come from somewhere else.

89
00:06:02,510 --> 00:06:06,936
The original repayment amount
stays the same in this example.

90
00:06:07,386 --> 00:06:10,867
Other claims on the
property can matter as well.

91
00:06:10,867 --> 00:06:16,885
If a home is sold at auction, there may be
a limited sum available to pay everyone.

92
00:06:16,885 --> 00:06:21,018
Who has priority can affect
how much the tenant recovers.

93
00:06:21,468 --> 00:06:25,085
Korea's housing lease law
provides some protections.

94
00:06:25,085 --> 00:06:28,703
For an ordinary housing
lease, taking possession and

95
00:06:28,703 --> 00:06:32,251
completing resident
registration can give the lease

96
00:06:32,251 --> 00:06:35,868
effect against third parties
from the following day.

97
00:06:36,318 --> 00:06:41,793
An officially assigned date on the
contract, along with those steps, can give

98
00:06:41,793 --> 00:06:46,557
the tenant priority over lower-ranking
claims in a qualifying sale.

99
00:06:46,557 --> 00:06:51,534
The order matters because several
claims can be tied to the same home.

100
00:06:51,984 --> 00:06:55,228
This is why existing
rights recorded against an

101
00:06:55,228 --> 00:06:59,162
apartment, such as a mortgage,
are relevant to the lease.

102
00:06:59,162 --> 00:07:02,888
The size of the deposit tells
you the amount at stake.

103
00:07:02,888 --> 00:07:05,856
It says little about
who ranks ahead of it.

104
00:07:06,306 --> 00:07:11,113
The lease law also says that the
lease relationship is deemed to

105
00:07:11,113 --> 00:07:15,620
continue after the term expires
until the deposit is repaid.

106
00:07:15,620 --> 00:07:20,202
That rule and the actual arrival
of money are separate steps.

107
00:07:20,652 --> 00:07:24,743
A different protection is
a deposit return guarantee.

108
00:07:24,743 --> 00:07:29,066
The Korea Housing and Urban
Guarantee Corporation, known

109
00:07:29,066 --> 00:07:33,389
as HUG, offers a product that
can cover repayment if the

110
00:07:33,389 --> 00:07:36,708
landlord fails to return
a covered deposit.

111
00:07:37,158 --> 00:07:41,629
A guarantee has eligibility
requirements and a claims process.

112
00:07:41,629 --> 00:07:44,875
Its place in the system
is to provide another

113
00:07:44,875 --> 00:07:48,264
route to repayment when
the conditions are met.

114
00:07:48,264 --> 00:07:51,798
The amount and timing
still matter to the tenant.

115
00:07:52,248 --> 00:07:55,987
The money and the keys move
on different schedules.

116
00:07:55,987 --> 00:08:00,240
The tenant uses the home while
the deposit is outstanding.

117
00:08:00,690 --> 00:08:05,100
And the large payment never turns
the tenant into a part owner.

118
00:08:05,100 --> 00:08:09,020
If the home's price rises
during the lease, the original

119
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:12,380
agreement still calls for
return of the deposit.

120
00:08:12,380 --> 00:08:15,810
It does not give the tenant
a share of that rise.

121
00:08:16,260 --> 00:08:18,350
2 questions explain jeonse.

122
00:08:18,350 --> 00:08:23,148
When does the money change hands,
and who has to give it back?

123
00:08:23,598 --> 00:08:29,195
The tenant exchanges access to a large
sum for a home with no monthly rent.

124
00:08:29,195 --> 00:08:33,822
The landlord gets that access
and takes on a future repayment.

125
00:08:34,272 --> 00:08:39,961
Back at that apartment door, the whole
agreement fits into a simple exchange.

126
00:08:39,961 --> 00:08:44,468
The keys come with a large
deposit, the monthly rent is zero,

127
00:08:44,468 --> 00:08:48,384
and when the lease ends the
same deposit is due back.
